WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:03.370
- [Narrator] Gordon
Ramsay headed to Norway

00:00:03.436 --> 00:00:04.637
to learn how to cook like a true
Viking

00:00:04.704 --> 00:00:09.109
But how this region developed
some of its traditional dishes

00:00:09.175 --> 00:00:12.846
happened long before these
explorers took to the seas.

00:00:12.912 --> 00:00:14.214
- I want to move to Norway.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.149
(laughing)

00:00:16.216 --> 00:00:18.818
(gentle music)

00:00:22.155 --> 00:00:23.990
- [Narrator] The Sámi
people are indigenous

00:00:24.057 --> 00:00:25.458
to the Sápmi region,

00:00:25.525 --> 00:00:27.694
which includes Northern parts of
Norway,

00:00:27.761 --> 00:00:30.363
Sweden, Finland, and Russia.

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:32.665
They're descendants of nomadic
peoples,

00:00:32.732 --> 00:00:35.668
who've been inhabited the
region for thousands of years.

00:00:36.569 --> 00:00:40.573
Their population today is
estimated to be around 80,000

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:43.443
with about half currently
living in Norway.

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:46.679
Despite the relatively
small population size,

00:00:46.746 --> 00:00:50.283
Sámi people have had a huge
influence on Nordic culture.

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:53.620
And even in feature
films like "Frozen II."

00:00:53.686 --> 00:00:57.457
But one of their biggest
influences is in their cuisine.

00:00:57.524 --> 00:01:00.126
Traditionally, the Sámi
were hunter-gatherers

00:01:00.193 --> 00:01:03.530
that feasted on in-season
berries and animal-based meals

00:01:03.596 --> 00:01:05.698
like reindeer meat and fish.

00:01:05.765 --> 00:01:08.701
The meats were preserved
through salting and drying.

00:01:08.768 --> 00:01:11.538
Communal reindeer herding
later replaced hunting

00:01:11.604 --> 00:01:14.541
and became an integral
part of the Sámi economy.

00:01:14.607 --> 00:01:15.875
- How many are in the herd?

00:01:15.942 --> 00:01:17.343
- What's your fortune?

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:19.312
- I think Magna is trying to
tell me

00:01:19.379 --> 00:01:22.982
that it's rude to ask a Sámi
about the size of their herd.

00:01:23.049 --> 00:01:23.950
- [Narrator] Culturally,

00:01:24.017 --> 00:01:26.453
the Sámi people used
all parts of the animal,

00:01:26.519 --> 00:01:28.455
so that nothing would go to
waste.

00:01:28.521 --> 00:01:29.289
- Are they warm?

00:01:29.355 --> 00:01:30.123
- Yes warm.

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:30.957
- That's amazing...

00:01:31.024 --> 00:01:32.358
So you waste nothing?

00:01:32.425 --> 00:01:34.060
- No, no, nothing at all--
- No waste even the sh--.

00:01:34.127 --> 00:01:36.563
I mean, could you make
them my size; size 15?

00:01:36.629 --> 00:01:37.597
- Ahh, expensive

00:01:37.664 --> 00:01:38.832
(laughing)

00:01:38.898 --> 00:01:40.967
- [Narrator] Animal byproducts
like hides, bones, and blood

00:01:41.034 --> 00:01:45.205
were used for clothing,
tools, and even other meals.

00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:47.740
blood pancakes anyone?

00:01:49.476 --> 00:01:50.543
- I actually like the flavor.

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:51.411
- Hmm.

00:01:51.478 --> 00:01:52.779
- The texture is quite thick and
dense.

00:01:52.846 --> 00:01:55.114
- [Narrator] And now onto the
Vikings.

00:01:55.181 --> 00:01:58.318
The Viking Age started
around the late 8th century

00:01:58.384 --> 00:02:01.221
where Danish, Norwegian,
and Swedish warriors

00:02:01.287 --> 00:02:04.791
took to the seas to raid and
colonize other parts of Europe.

00:02:04.858 --> 00:02:07.894
to meet the high energy
demands of a Viking lifestyle,

00:02:07.961 --> 00:02:09.929
high-fat foods were a necessity

00:02:09.996 --> 00:02:12.165
especially in winter months.

00:02:12.232 --> 00:02:14.767
Vikings were comprised
of independent farmers,

00:02:14.834 --> 00:02:17.437
and when on land, they
surprisingly had a good amount

00:02:17.504 --> 00:02:19.205
of variety in their diet.

00:02:19.272 --> 00:02:23.877
This included cereals, animal
milks, wild fruit and berries

00:02:23.943 --> 00:02:27.747
and a wide variety of meat,
including horses, pigs

00:02:27.814 --> 00:02:29.649
goats and sheep.

00:02:29.716 --> 00:02:31.618
- This is a sheep head.

00:02:32.485 --> 00:02:34.487
- How far does this date back?

00:02:34.554 --> 00:02:36.256
- [9th] century.

00:02:36.322 --> 00:02:38.091
- Vikings.
- Wow.

00:02:38.157 --> 00:02:42.829
Fish and fresh shellfish, like
scallops were also staples

00:02:42.896 --> 00:02:44.564
both at land, and at sea.

00:02:44.631 --> 00:02:46.833
- I'm amazed how sweet they are.

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:48.201
That's a big shock for me.

00:02:48.268 --> 00:02:50.870
- [Narrator] Like the Sámi,
Vikings were not known to let

00:02:50.937 --> 00:02:51.971
food go to waste.

00:02:52.038 --> 00:02:54.707
Domestic animals were first
used as working animals

00:02:54.774 --> 00:02:58.578
then later eaten which led
to established delicacies

00:02:58.645 --> 00:02:59.846
like sheep's head.

00:02:59.913 --> 00:03:01.814
Boiling meat in stews was a
great way

00:03:01.881 --> 00:03:04.083
It helped a meal stretch
and stay flavorful.

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:06.619
For example in a Viking Skause

00:03:06.686 --> 00:03:09.322
meats and vegetables were
taking out of the pot

00:03:09.389 --> 00:03:11.357
then replaced with new ones.

00:03:11.424 --> 00:03:13.927
This allowed the broth
to be extra concentrated

00:03:13.993 --> 00:03:15.328
throughout the days of cooking.

00:03:15.395 --> 00:03:18.097
Also like the Sámi, meat and
fish were

00:03:18.164 --> 00:03:21.601
often dried and salted for
long-term preservation.

00:03:21.668 --> 00:03:24.537
From the indigenous
traditions of the Sámi,

00:03:24.604 --> 00:03:26.673
To the conquering spirits of the
Vikings,

00:03:26.739 --> 00:03:29.809
Norwegian cuisine and food
preparation has survived

00:03:29.876 --> 00:03:30.677
for centuries.

00:03:30.743 --> 00:03:32.679
And while some of the dishes may
look

00:03:32.745 --> 00:03:34.547
a little different today

00:03:34.614 --> 00:03:38.017
Their roots will remain
for generations to come.

